This is what keeps the system feeling like a single organism rather than a bag of parts.

4. Time evolution as reversible encryption
4.1 Evolution operator
Define a global operator:
[ E_K: S_t \rightarrow S_{t+1} ]
(S_t): Universe state at time (t)
(K): Key (could encode rule set, parameters, boundary conditions)
Requirement: (E_K) is invertible:
[ E_K^{-1}(S_{t+1}) = S_t ]
4.2 Implementation patterns
Cellular automaton style (reversible CA):
Use known reversible CA patterns (e.g., Margolus neighborhood, partitioned CA).
Each step is a permutation of local configurations.
Block-cipher style:
Partition the state into blocks (regions).
Apply:
Substitution: Local nonlinear transforms.
Permutation: Shuffle indices/regions.
Multiple rounds per “time step” if needed.
Key schedule:
Static key: Same rules each step.
Dynamic key: Key evolves with time or with state (self-referential dynamics).
4.3 Time control
Forward step: Apply E_K.
Backward step: Apply E_K^{-1}.
Jumping:
Use checkpoints (snapshots of UniverseState) plus reversible steps to interpolate.
Or treat time index as a parameter in the key schedule and recompute deterministically.

