IV. Encryption‑based time‑evolution algorithm (in detail)
1. Conceptual structure
You treat each time step as a cipher round:
Plaintext: current universe state ( S_t )
Ciphertext: next universe state ( S_{t+1} )
Key: ( K_t = \text{KeySchedule}(t) )
Round function: ( E_{K_t} ) (reversible)
You require:
[ S_{t+1} = E_{K_t}(S_t), \quad S_t = E_{K_t}^{-1}(S_{t+1}) ]
2. State encoding
Represent UniverseState as:
Topology (fixed or slowly changing).
Fields (values per node/edge).
For encryption‑style operations, you can:
Flatten field values into a vector.
Or operate directly on structured data with consistent indexing.
3. Round function structure
Each round ( E_{K_t} ) is composed of:
Local substitution step
For each node (or cell), apply a nonlinear transform:
( v'i = f(v_i, {v_j}{j \in N(i)}, K_t) )
Implemented as reversible rule(s) (e.g., XOR, modular add/sub, reversible logic).
Global permutation step
Permute indices of nodes or field components:
( v'' = P_{K_t}(v') )
Permutation is a bijection; inverse is ( P_{K_t}^{-1} ).
Constraint‑aware adjustment (optional)
Apply reversible corrections to enforce conservation/symmetry:
E.g., pairwise swaps, modular balancing.
Overall:
[ E_{K_t} = C_{K_t} \circ P_{K_t} \circ F_{K_t} ]
Where:
( F_{K_t} ): local substitution
( P_{K_t} ): permutation
( C_{K_t} ): constraint correction (also reversible)
4. Inverse round
To reverse:
[ E_{K_t}^{-1} = F_{K_t}^{-1} \circ P_{K_t}^{-1} \circ C_{K_t}^{-1} ]
You implement:
forward() applying F -> P -> C
backward() applying C^{-1} -> P^{-1} -> F^{-1}
Because each substep is explicitly reversible, the whole is reversible.
5. Pseudocode for one step
def round_forward(state: UniverseState, key: Key) -> UniverseState:
    s1 = apply_local_substitution(state, key)   # F_K
    s2 = apply_permutation(s1, key)            # P_K
    s3 = apply_constraint_correction(s2, key)  # C_K
    return s3


def round_backward(state: UniverseState, key: Key) -> UniverseState:
    s1 = apply_constraint_correction_inverse(state, key)  # C_K^{-1}
    s2 = apply_permutation_inverse(s1, key)               # P_K^{-1}
    s3 = apply_local_substitution_inverse(s2, key)        # F_K^{-1}
    return s3
6. Integration with EvolutionEngine
Forward:
Compute key = key_schedule(t).
Apply each rule’s forward(state, key) in sequence.
Backward:
Compute key = key_schedule(t-1).
Apply each rule’s backward(state, key) in reverse sequence.
This gives you:
Exact time reversibility.
Key‑dependent evolution (encryption).
A clean mapping between time control in the UI and cipher rounds in the engine.


